Steps:-
Step 1: Install Wireshark
Install Wireshark on the Windows computer. During installation, install Npcap when prompted because it is
required for packet capture.
Screenshot
Step 2: Open Wireshark
Open Wireshark. The main window displays the available network interfaces such as Wi-Fi and Ethernet.
Screenshot
Step 3: Select the Active Network Interface
Identify the network interface that is currently connected to the network. For example, select Wi-Fi if the
computer is connected through Wi-Fi.
Screenshot
Step 4: Start Packet Capture
Double-click the active network interface. Wireshark will start capturing network packets.
Screenshot
Step 5: Generate Test Traffic
Open Command Prompt and execute:
ping google.com
Alternatively, you can ping a permitted device on your own local network, such as the default gateway.
Screenshot
Step 6: Detect ICMP Traffic
In the Wireshark display filter bar, enter the following filter and press Enter:
icmp
The captured packets will show ICMP Echo Request and Echo Reply packets.
Screenshot
Step 7: Detect TCP Connection Attempts
Use the following display filter:
tcp.flags.syn == 1
This displays TCP SYN packets used during TCP connection establishment. Repeated connection attempts to
many ports may require further investigation.
Screenshot
Step 8: Detect DNS Traffic
Use:
dns
This displays DNS traffic. The source, destination, domain name and query information can be examined.
Screenshot
To display DNS queries, use:
dns.flags.response == 0
Screenshot
Step 9: Detect HTTP Traffic
Use:
http
If HTTP traffic is present, Wireshark can display HTTP requests and responses. Modern websites generally use
HTTPS, so readable HTTP application data may not be available.
Screenshot
Step 10: Detect ARP Traffic
Use:
arp
ARP packets can be examined to identify IP-to-MAC address resolution activity. Unexpected ARP behavior can
be investigated as a possible security event.
Screenshot
Step 11: Analyze Possible Port-Scanning Activity
In an authorized lab environment, examine TCP SYN traffic using:
tcp.flags.syn == 1
Screenshot
Check whether one source repeatedly sends SYN packets to many different destination ports. Such behavior
may indicate port-scanning activity and should be investigated.
Step 12: Analyze Possible ICMP Flooding
Apply the filter:
icmp
Observe the number of ICMP packets during the capture. An unusually high volume of packets may require
further investigation.
